1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,961
Before you merge cells or coarsen a netlist, you need a score that says these two

2
00:00:04,961 --> 00:00:06,247
should stay together.

3
00:00:06,247 --> 00:00:07,655
That score is affinity.

4
00:00:07,655 --> 00:00:12,739
In this module you’ll compare plain edge weight with shared-neighbor scoring on the

5
00:00:12,739 --> 00:00:15,923
same tiny graph, and watch who wants to merge first.

6
00:00:15,923 --> 00:00:20,884
Then you’ll see a graph where the two scores disagree, and a third score for real

7
00:00:20,884 --> 00:00:23,640
netlists, where one net can touch many cells.

8
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:26,638
Five cells A through E with edge weights.

9
00:00:26,638 --> 00:00:32,050
Heavy edges A, B and D, E both weigh five; the bridge through C is weaker.

10
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:36,072
Affinity will decide which pairs want to cluster first.

11
00:00:36,072 --> 00:00:40,436
Pure edge-weight affinity just sorts existing edges.

12
00:00:40,436 --> 00:00:44,884
A, B and D, E tie at five; tie-break puts A, B first.

13
00:00:44,884 --> 00:00:48,576
That ranking is the simplest merge priority.

14
00:00:48,576 --> 00:00:51,428
A and B already share edge weight five.

15
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:53,402
They also share neighbor C.

16
00:00:53,402 --> 00:00:59,252
Shared-neighbor affinity adds the minimum of those spokes, one, so A, B rises to

17
00:00:59,252 --> 00:00:59,544
six.

18
00:00:59,544 --> 00:01:03,876
Under shared-neighbor scoring, A, B and D, E lead at six.

19
00:01:03,876 --> 00:01:07,068
Weak edge A, C jumps to five because of B.

20
00:01:07,068 --> 00:01:11,400
Non-edges like B, D can appear from shared support alone.

21
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:16,832
Affinity is the objective’s local preference: which cells belong together before

22
00:01:16,832 --> 00:01:18,122
global constraints.

23
00:01:18,122 --> 00:01:22,128
Bad affinity means bad merges, and harder refinement later.

24
00:01:22,128 --> 00:01:24,949
Here is the shared-neighbor score as code.

25
00:01:24,949 --> 00:01:28,644
First, every existing edge adds its weight to its pair.

26
00:01:28,644 --> 00:01:34,085
Then, for every pair of nodes, look at each neighbor they have in common, and add

27
00:01:34,085 --> 00:01:36,906
the smaller of the two connecting weights.

28
00:01:36,906 --> 00:01:40,332
If a pair ends up with a score above zero, keep it.

29
00:01:40,332 --> 00:01:45,841
Finally, sort by score, highest first, and break ties alphabetically, so your list

30
00:01:45,841 --> 00:01:47,856
comes out the same every time.

31
00:01:47,856 --> 00:01:53,700
Under shared-neighbor scoring the starter graph produces ten pairs, not six.

32
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:56,237
Four of them have no edge at all.

33
00:01:56,237 --> 00:02:01,236
B and D both connect to C, so B, D scores the smaller spoke, two.

34
00:02:01,236 --> 00:02:04,850
A, D, A, E and B, E each pick up one through C.

35
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:10,924
These pairs aren't mistakes: they say two cells are close even without a direct

36
00:02:10,924 --> 00:02:11,309
wire.

37
00:02:11,309 --> 00:02:15,384
A pair is only left out when its score would be zero.

38
00:02:15,384 --> 00:02:17,770
Here's why the choice matters.

39
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:24,370
Take a tight triangle P, Q, R, with every edge at weight four, and a lone pair X, Y

40
00:02:24,370 --> 00:02:25,563
at weight five.

41
00:02:25,563 --> 00:02:29,857
Edge weight picks X, Y first, because five beats four.

42
00:02:29,857 --> 00:02:35,185
Shared-neighbor scoring gives P, Q four plus four through R: eight.

43
00:02:35,185 --> 00:02:39,081
X, Y has no common neighbor, so it stays at five.

44
00:02:39,081 --> 00:02:43,296
Same graph, same merge engine, different first merge.

45
00:02:43,296 --> 00:02:45,813
Real netlists aren't simple edges.

46
00:02:45,813 --> 00:02:50,478
One net can connect many cells, like a reset or an enable line.

47
00:02:50,478 --> 00:02:56,623
If you count every shared net at full weight, cells A and B look close just because

48
00:02:56,623 --> 00:02:57,733
both see reset.

49
00:02:57,733 --> 00:02:59,880
Pin-count scoring fixes this.

50
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:05,581
Each net splits its weight across its pins: weight divided by pins minus one.

51
00:03:05,581 --> 00:03:07,580
A two-pin net counts fully.

52
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:11,208
A five-pin net gives only a quarter to each pair.

53
00:03:11,208 --> 00:03:13,720
In the browser lab, you do the scoring.

54
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:18,810
Click two nodes to pick a pair, even a pair with no edge, work out its score by

55
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:20,163
hand, and type it in.

56
00:03:20,163 --> 00:03:25,509
You can also build your own ranking, one pair at a time, and change edge weights to

57
00:03:25,509 --> 00:03:27,313
see which pair merges first.

58
00:03:27,313 --> 00:03:31,114
You can switch between all three graphs and all the scores.

59
00:03:31,114 --> 00:03:35,559
The ten challenges check what you typed, your ranking and your edits.

60
00:03:35,559 --> 00:03:40,906
The breakdown and the full ranking are there for study, but anything they reveal no

61
00:03:40,906 --> 00:03:41,808
longer counts.

62
00:03:41,808 --> 00:03:46,693
In the implement track, print both rankings for the starter graph, then for the

63
00:03:46,693 --> 00:03:50,218
priority graph, and compare the first line of each table.

64
00:03:50,218 --> 00:03:55,042
Then write your own scoring functions, including pin-count, and run the golden

65
00:03:55,042 --> 00:03:56,341
checker on your file.

66
00:03:56,341 --> 00:04:01,102
It checks all three scores, and it feeds the edges in a scrambled order, so a

67
00:04:01,102 --> 00:04:03,576
missing tie-break shows up as a failure.

68
00:04:03,576 --> 00:04:05,155
Watch for four things.

69
00:04:05,155 --> 00:04:09,031
Counting an undirected edge twice, once from each end.

70
00:04:09,031 --> 00:04:14,702
Adding the larger spoke instead of the smaller one, which makes everything look

71
00:04:14,702 --> 00:04:15,133
close.

72
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:20,229
Forgetting the tie-break, so A, B and D, E swap places from run to run.

73
00:04:20,229 --> 00:04:25,971
And using raw net counts on a netlist, where wide nets like reset glue unrelated

74
00:04:25,971 --> 00:04:27,048
cells together.

75
00:04:27,048 --> 00:04:32,301
Match all three golden checks, then explain two things in your own words: why A, C

76
00:04:32,301 --> 00:04:37,554
climbs under shared-neighbor scoring, and why the priority graph changes its first

77
00:04:37,554 --> 00:04:37,938
merge.

78
00:04:37,938 --> 00:04:43,191
Take the short quiz, then continue to greedy pair merge, where these scores become

79
00:04:43,191 --> 00:04:44,280
the merge policy.
